  animalName: Stegosaurus
  scientificName: Stegosaurus stenops
  classification: Plated Herbivore (Plant Eater)
  era: Late Jurassic (~155 - 150 Million Years Ago)
  averageLength: 9.0 meters (30 ft)
  averageWeight: 5,000 kg (5 metric tons)
  description: Stegosaurus inhabited the lush floodplains of Late Jurassic North
    America millions of years earlier, around 155 to 150 million years ago, making
    it a prominent slow-moving herbivore of its era. Easily recognized by the double
    row of large, flat, kite-shaped bony plates lining its arched back, Stegosaurus
    used these vascularized structures primarily for body temperature regulation,
    species recognition, or intimidating rivals by flushing them with blood. Despite
    having a relatively tiny brain relative to its massive 30-foot body, it possessed
    a formidable physical defense at the tip of its flexible tail. Known as a thagomizer,
    this natural weapon featured four long, dangerously sharp bony spikes that Stegosaurus
    could swing sideways with immense force to ward off apex predators like Allosaurus.
  funFact: Despite being the size of a bus, Stegosaurus had a brain the size of a
    single walnut (weighing only about 80 grams)!
